Disney's wreckit-ralph was a veritable  Easter Eggstravaganza for video game  nerds especially those that spent half  their lives in the arcade during the 80s  and 90s here's a look at some of the  things in the film only adults will have  noticed there's a lot going on with the  two policeman characters introduced in  the sugar rush realm the pair serve the  tyrannical King candy meaning they're  pretty fast and loose with their use of  tasers and batons which reads a bit like  a statement in itself especially when  factoring in the surge protectors random  screening stops with Ralph's  random Security check random I behind  you always stop me I'm just a surge  protector doing my job sir not to  mention their motto is to heat and serve  which is pretty close to the usual law  enforcement credo of to protect and  serve  on a lighter note they're also shaped  like a donut and an eclair and our name  Winchell and Duncan as an homage to  Winchell's donuts and Dunkin Donuts to  children they may seem like any other of  the personified edibles running around  the game but their parents are sure to  get a chuckle out of this somewhat  unsubtle play on the whole cops like  doughnuts stereotype generations ears  might be inclined to fixate on some of  the more contemporary references  scattered about the screen in wreck-it  Ralph things like dance dance revelution  Bowser from Super Mario Brothers and the  halo asked soldiers rift on in hero's  duty but there are some background  characters with much longer histories  Frogger Street Fighter and Sonic the  Hedgehog are probably still mainstream  enough to ring a bell for most people  some of the more obscure characters in  the film however include tappers  bowtie-wearing bartender and Qbert and  while fully grown film goers might find  their fingers glued to the pause button  as they scour the screens for these  hidden gems the younger crowd probably  doesn't care about a bunch of 8-bit  oldies even the most obvious game  reference of all in wreck-it Ralph could  be easily missed by anyone who wasn't  around for the glory days of the  earliest arcade systems fix-it felix is  purely a cinematic creation but was  undoubtedly inspired by Donkey Kong  which debuted in 1981 on an arcade  machine that also featured the debut of  jump man otherwise known as Mario Donkey  Kong as a character will still be  familiar to today's device wielding  whippersnappers since he appears in  modern game  like Mario Kart and Super Smash Brothers  so it's probably not too surprising that  his hulking size brute strength and  general officious inspired a lot of  Ralph's physical and personality traits  its fix-it felix is mission and play  style that really bears a striking  resemblance to the old Donkey Kong  though  [Music]  anyhoo what else I'm a wrecker I wrecked  things professionally in both games the  do-gooder is charged with climbing a  large structure while the villain tosses  debris down at them they might not be  exactly the same but well they  practically are it's not until the tail  end of wreckit-ralph that audiences find  out the film features another Disney  Princess to add to the collection but  even after she suited up in a ball gown  vanellope von schweetz handles this fact  very differently than her forebears  sure there are a few theoretical  similarities to the princesses of old  she's been deprived of her title by an  evildoer gets bullied by a bunch of Mean  Girls and is frequently told that her  eccentricities in this case glitches are  troublesome so far so Disney Princess  unlike her peers however vanellope has  no interest in wearing the crown even  Matta for the most part there's nothing  stereotypically princessy about her  she's feisty resourceful determined and  has absolutely no interest in living a  prim life of privilege not only that but  there's nary a love interest inside  either  and while Ralph does help her fulfill  her dream of earning a spot in the big  race she does a lot of the legwork  herself the diet coke and mentos geyser  gag that saves the day in wreck-it Ralph  is based on a real trend that became big  in the mid Ott's after an on-air  demonstration of the effect went viral  subsequently inspiring an episode of  Mythbusters for those who grew up ahead  of the internet age though there was a  much earlier version of the messy  at-home experiment that everyone just  had to try way back when it was the  wintergreen flavored lifesavers that  kids would drop into their soda bottles  to set off a massive liquid eruption  that was just one of the many food item  combination challenges that would get  around by word of mouth in the 90s so  while the candy soda combo trick in  wreck-it Ralph may be a convenient plot  device that kids can shrug their  shoulders at the older crowd might just  get a tinge of nostalgia after seeing it  coming to use  [Music]
 

